Orthostatic Intolerance (Undifferentiated)
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Symptom provocation strictly upon upright posture | Rapid relief upon recumbency | Absence of classical blood pressure drops (orthostatic hypotension)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-014
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe intolerance to standing resulting in profound weakness and tachycardia, requiring urgent exclusion of acute pulmonary embolism or myocarditis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset positional tachycardia, tremulousness, and an inability to remain upright. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out acute cardiopulmonary compromise, preventing further hemodynamic instability prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Whenever I stand up, my heart pounds out of my chest, I start shaking all over, and my legs feel like lead.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports severe lightheadedness upon assuming an upright posture with a sensation of imminent syncope, though without actual loss of consciousness. Standing triggers palpitations, generalized weakness, and leg heaviness. The patient complains of diminished cognitive concentration (brain fog) and vague abdominal discomfort triggered strictly by orthostasis. There is tremulousness and subjective shakiness, with rapid, complete symptom relief upon returning to a supine position. Symptoms are notably exacerbated in warm environments.
Functional Status: Confined to bed due to orthostatic tachycardia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to perform basic instrumental activities of daily living.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Supine 112/68 mmHg, Standing 110/66 mmHg | HR: Supine 74 bpm, Standing 118 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious and tremulous when standing, reverting to a calm baseline when supine. Review of systemic history reveals profound chronic fatigue, episodes of pica (pagophagia - ice chewing), and previous lab results showing low ferritin with normal hemoglobin (iron deficiency without anemia).
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a rapidly accelerating sinus tachycardia upon standing without a corresponding drop in blood pressure. There are no murmurs or gallops. Respiratory exam is completely clear. Neurological exam reveals a fine postural tremor when upright, which resolves when supine. No focal motor or sensory deficits.
Environmental Observations: Ambient room temperature is excessively high.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes no regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Orthostatic Intolerance (Undifferentiated) - Status: Acute exacerbation of autonomic dysfunction.
Differentials:

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out due to the strictly positional nature of the tachycardia, normal room air oxygen saturation, and absence of pleuritic chest pain.

Orthostatic Hypotension - Ruled out due to the objective maintenance of blood pressure upon standing despite the severe tachycardic response.

Acute Thyrotoxicosis - Ruled out due to the absence of resting tachycardia, normal thyroid gland on palpation, and immediate resolution of symptoms when supine.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Functional impairment).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This urgent evaluation was necessary to assess profound postural tachycardia and an inability to remain upright, raising suspicion for acute cardiopulmonary pathology. Telephone triage identified the acute risk for evolving pulmonary embolism or structural cardiac failure. Detailed orthostatic provocation testing confirmed isolated, extreme heart rate acceleration without hypotension or hypoxia, strictly tied to gravity. This physical confirmation completely ruled out life-threatening acute ischemia or respiratory failure. The assessment safely secured the patient in their home by diagnosing a severe but hemodynamically stable autonomic response, preventing an unnecessary, distress-inducing emergency department transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated aggressive oral fluid and salt loading protocols. The expected investigation results include a heart rate increment exceeding 30 beats per minute or a sustained blood pressure drop upon active standing during a formal tilt table test, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via a cardiologist.
Positive Behavioral Change: To increase blood volume and vascular tone, consume a large, heavily salted glass of water immediately upon waking before getting out of bed; actively pre-loading your fluid volume will directly blunt the massive heart rate spike when you stand.
Home-Based Services: None indicated.
DME/Safety: Prescribed thigh-high compression stockings to reduce venous pooling.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for cardiology referral and formal iron infusion consideration.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops chest pain that does not resolve with lying down, true loss of consciousness, or severe difficulty breathing at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; autonomic conditions require extensive formal testing to type correctly (e.g., POTS).
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves chronic, fluctuating intolerance to standing, requiring strict adherence to lifestyle modifications, fluid management, and potential pharmacological control.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if your heart continues to race violently even after you have been lying flat for more than ten minutes.

